% ============================================================================
% mitosis-phase-order — each phase of mitosis as a NUMBER (1-4), the position
% in the ordered cycle (adj-facts-stdlib, BIOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% `biology/mitosis-phases.adj` already tables each phase's defining EVENT
% (prophase → chromatin_forms_chromosomes, etc.) and documents in its own
% header that the ordering is asserted by the page as an ORDERED LIST, whose
% lead-in the page states as "The four phases of mitosis are" — but that
% ordering lives only in the row
% ORDER of that table, not as a queryable NUMBER. This sibling table makes
% the same source's ordering a first-class, bindable fact, exactly the shape
% `astronomy/moon-phases.adj` already established for `moon_phase_order`
% (phase → number, so a recalled position composes into arithmetic or, as
% `mitosis-phase-ordinal-position.adj` does, into an ordinal WORD). Recall is
% a binding query:
%
%     ? mitosis_phase_order(anaphase, $N)   % 3
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `mitosis_phase_order(phase, order)` carrying the NCI SEER citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% position WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains
% on `interphase` (the resting phase BETWEEN divisions, deliberately not one
% of the four ordered phases, matching `mitosis-phases.adj`'s own exclusion).
%
% Provenance: the page's own lead-in, quoted contiguously and NOTHING MORE —
% "The four phases of mitosis are" — which is byte-exact in the raw HTML and
% occurs exactly once. On the page that paragraph is followed immediately by
% an ORDERED LIST, `<ol class="usa-list">`, whose four top-level items are,
% in document order: Prophase, Metaphase, Anaphase, and "Telophase (divided
% into parts I and II)".
%
% THE ORDER THIS TABLE ENCODES IS READ FROM THAT LIST STRUCTURE, NOT FROM ANY
% SENTENCE. No contiguous span of the page's text states the four phases in
% sequence, because each phase's label is separated from the next by that
% phase's own nested sub-list of events. An `<ol>` genuinely does assert an
% ordering — that is what the element means — but it asserts it as STRUCTURE,
% and the `source` field holds text. So the field says what the page says in
% one piece, and this comment says where the rest comes from. That is the
% house answer installment 4c established on solfège and 4i applied again on
% shape-composition: quote the real span, disclose the composition, never
% smuggle the composition inside the quotation marks.
%
% WHAT THIS FIELD USED TO SAY, and why it is worth writing down: "The four
% phases of mitosis are Prophase ... Metaphase ... Anaphase ... Telophase."
% That string occurs ZERO times on the page — not in the raw HTML, not in the
% tag-stripped text, not after whitespace collapsing. THREE separate things
% were invented. (1) The " ... " separator appears nowhere on the page; it is
% an editorial mark standing in for the list items, sitting inside quotation
% marks where it reads as the page's own words. (2) The terminal period is
% not there — the paragraph ends at "are", because it is a lead-in to a list
% and not a sentence. (3) The fourth item is not the bare word "Telophase"
% but "Telophase (divided into parts I and II)", a qualifier this file's own
% four-row table deliberately does not model. The old text also called this
% "a sentence already verified for the sibling table" — `mitosis-phases.adj`
% ships the CHROMATIN sentence, which is byte-exact; the composite appears
% there only inside its own comments, so nothing had verified it anywhere.
%
% This does NOT decide whether an ordered-list item is a verbatim span. That
% question is the same family as the held table-row one (#13934) and stays
% open; the repair only removes what was invented and quotes the paragraph,
% which is indisputably one real span.
%
% `trust authoritative` — the U.S. National Cancer Institute's own teaching
% page.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts, feedback_adjudication_no_interpretive_gating.)
% ============================================================================

table mitosis_phase_order {
    columns phase, order

    row (prophase, 1)
    row (metaphase, 2)
    row (anaphase, 3)
    row (telophase, 4)

    source "The four phases of mitosis are"
    locator "https://training.seer.cancer.gov/disease/cancer/biology/cycle.html"
    trust authoritative
}
